.Dd September 30, 2026
.Dt NOVI-SANDBOX 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm novi-sandbox
.Nd run a program that cannot reach the machine
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl -ro Ar path ...
.Op Fl -rw Ar path ...
.Op Fl -no-net
.Op Fl -profile Ar name
.Op Fl -
.Ar command Op Ar argument ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
runs
.Ar command
with a root filesystem containing only the paths named on its command
line, its own process table, and a seccomp filter.
It needs no privilege: the other namespaces are created as a side
effect of a user namespace the caller owns.
.Bl -tag -width "--profile name"
.It Fl -ro Ar path
Make
.Ar path
visible, read-only.
.It Fl -rw Ar path
Make
.Ar path
visible and writable.
.It Fl -no-net
An empty network namespace.
.Sy Not the default :
a browser needs the network, and a tool that quietly took it away
would break the first caller.
.It Fl -profile Ar name
An
.Sy allowlist
filter instead of the default denylist, for a program whose syscall
set somebody measured.
.Nm
with no arguments lists the profiles and their sizes.
.El
.Ss Default deny, named on the command line
The new root is an empty
tmpfs;
a path nobody named is not there.
The alternative
.Pf \(em Nm mount No the real root and over-mount the sensitive parts
\(em is a denylist of
.Em directories ,
so every future package that puts something private somewhere new is
a hole nobody opens a file to notice.
.Ss What the filter is, and is not
The default filter is a
.Sy denylist ,
and that is a deliberate limit rather than an oversight.
An allowlist is stronger and has to know every syscall the program
and its libc will ever make; being wrong turns a working browser into
a crash on a page nobody tested.
A refused call returns
.Er EPERM
rather than killing the process, because a killed process tells the
person nothing.
.Pp
So: this is confinement, not a proof.
Do not let the word
.Dq seccomp
supply the stronger meaning.
.Ss Two layers, two errnos
A bind mount cannot say
.Dq writable but not executable ,
so Landlock is the second layer: it grants execute on the
.Fl -ro
paths and never on the
.Fl -rw
ones, and the mounts this program makes itself carry
.Dv MS_NOEXEC .
Measured, same file, one flag apart: a
.Fl -rw
path reads and writes and cannot exec
.Pq Er EACCES ;
a
.Fl -ro
path reads and execs and cannot write
.Pq Er EROFS .
Neither layer can state the other's rule, which is the argument for
both.
.Ss Signals
The program is pid 1 of its own PID namespace, and the kernel gives
such a process the protection it gives the machine's own init: a
signal still at
.Dv SIG_DFL
coming from outside the namespace is
.Sy discarded ,
and
kill(1)
returns 0 regardless.
.Nm
therefore supervises: it forwards signals, and makes the
.Em second
one
.Dv SIGKILL ,
because a first forward may be thrown away and from outside that is
indistinguishable from a clean shutdown.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width "NOVI_SANDBOX_DESCRIBE" -compact
.It Ev NOVI_SANDBOX_DESCRIBE
Set to 1 in front of a sandboxed program's
.Em wrapper
to print the argv that wrapper would build, with every runtime branch
taken, instead of running it.
This is what
.Nm novi-agent describe
reads, so its answer cannot drift from what actually execs.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Nm
exits with the program's status.
It exits 2 on a usage error and 125 if the sandbox could not be set
up, naming which step failed.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Read one file and nothing else, off the network:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ novi-sandbox --no-net --ro /usr/bin/novi-view --ro /lib \e
      --ro "$(readlink -f photo.png)" -- novi-view photo.png
.Ed
.Pp
See what a sandboxed program is actually run with:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ NOVI_SANDBOX_DESCRIBE=1 novi-recon
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr novi-agent 1
.Sh STANDARDS
RFC 0039.
.Sh CAVEATS
A sandbox changes what a failing program can
.Em reach ,
not whether it fails or what failing costs.
Measured against a hostile-page corpus: identical survival, identical
peak memory.
What a layout engine allocates is its own heap and no mount list
touches it.
